Here is a complete, modern implementation using **Ember Octane** (ES Modules, `@ember/trackable`, and `@ember/component-lifecycle`).

### Prerequisites

Ensure you have a standard Ember Octane setup. If you need to install the worker polyfill (for older browsers) or specific helpers, you can add them, but standard modern browsers support `Worker` natively.

### 1. The Worker File (`workers/csv-parser.worker.js`)

This file runs in a separate thread. It receives the file content, parses it, and sends the result back to the main thread.

```javascript
// app/workers/csv-parser.worker.js

self.onmessage = (event) => {
  const { fileContent, delimiter = ',' } = event.data;

  try {
    // Basic CSV parsing logic
    // Note: For production, consider using a library like PapaParse
    const lines = fileContent.split(/\r\n|\n/);
    const headers = lines[0].split(delimiter).map((h) => h.trim());
    const rows = lines.slice(1).map((line) => {
      const values = line.split(delimiter).map((v) => v.trim());
      return headers.map((header, index) => ({
        [header]: values[index] || '',
      }));
    });

    // Send result back to main thread
    self.postMessage({
      type: 'success',
      data: { headers, rows },
    });
  } catch (error) {
    self.postMessage({
      type: 'error',
      message: error.message,
    });
  }
};
```

